  "guidance": "CORE POSITIONING: Digital Leads Co helps plumbing businesses improve the connection between their Google Business Profile, website, local search presence and customer enquiry process. Lead with a specific, verified observation from the public GBP + website review. Do not sell 'a website' before you understand the business priority.\n\nPRIMARY OUTCOME: A 10–15 minute walkthrough of the short audit / selected-business Growth Preview, or a clearly agreed callback. Do not force a close on a cold call. If the prospect is ready to buy, qualify fit and scope before proposal.\n\nAUDIT-FIRST METHOD: Always carry two useful findings: (1) one public Google Business Profile observation, (2) one website/customer-journey observation. Show the source and explain the possible customer impact in plain English. Clearly label quick public checks as a snapshot, not a comprehensive SEO audit. Never claim access to private GBP insights, Analytics, Search Console, call records, conversion data, customer records, revenue or lead counts unless the owner has granted appropriate access. Never say they are losing jobs or that competitors are taking their work. A competitor comparison must use verifiable public information and a fair comparison.\n\nSALES LOGIC: diagnose before prescribing; ask one question at a time; listen; reflect the prospect's words; make one relevant recommendation; explain deliverables and boundaries; ask for a small next step. Do not dump every package into the call. Avoid pressure, fake scarcity, artificial urgency, unsupported ROI, guaranteed rankings, guaranteed leads, and unsolicited discounting. If there is no genuine need, say so and close politely.\n\nPACKAGE FIT: Free Growth Preview is selective and is not a full audit or implementation. Complete Local SEO Audit A$299 is diagnostic only; implementation is separate. Website Launch A$1,990 is a max-five-page professional website and basic on-page SEO, not a complete Local SEO package. Local Visibility Foundation A$3,490 is the core one-off website + local SEO foundation for one GBP location. Local Market Expansion starts at A$5,490 for larger service/area coverage. Local Care A$149/month is maintenance, not SEO growth. Local SEO Growth A$749/month and Local SEO Expansion A$1,149/month are ongoing optimisation options for an existing, serviceable foundation. Business Growth Automation is custom-quoted after technical discovery. Recommend the smallest suitable scope; don't stack services unnecessarily.\n\nPRICE INTEGRITY: State the listed AUD prices when asked. Do not hide known pricing behind vague language. Do not invent whether GST is included, payment milestones, discounts, hosting/domain charges, third-party costs, or contract terms. Confirm all commercial terms, deliverables, exclusions, ownership and payment terms in a written proposal. Expansion is a starting price; Automation is custom quote.\n\nAUSTRALIAN LANGUAGE: Use natural, plain Australian English and Australian spelling: enquiries, optimise, prioritise, organisation, arvo (sparingly), suburbs, quote, call-out, trade business. Normal phrases include 'No worries', 'Fair enough', 'That makes sense', 'Have you got 30 seconds?', 'Would it be worth a look?', 'What sort of work are you keen to get more of?', 'Which suburbs matter most?', 'When's usually quieter for you?', 'Appreciate your time'. Do not force slang, fake an Australian accent, overuse 'mate', or use US-centric wording such as 'estimate' where 'quote' is more natural. Prefer 'job', 'enquiry', 'quote request', 'service area', 'Google Business Profile', and 'investment' when describing a real business asset. Use 'price' or 'cost' when clarity matters; 'investment' is not a substitute for honest pricing.\n\nWORDS TO PREFER: more relevant enquiries; quote requests; local customers; get found on Google; clearer service pages; areas you cover; easier to call or request a quote; local visibility; service-area coverage; customer enquiry path; one-off investment (for one-off work); ongoing monthly service (for retainers).\n\nWORDS TO AVOID: cheap/cheapest; guaranteed #1; guaranteed leads; skyrocket; dominate Google; flood your phone; you are losing customers; your website is terrible; we are the best; no-brainer; only takes a minute; digital transformation; technical jargon without a plain-English explanation. Never replace every use of 'cost' with 'investment'; use clear price language.\n\nOBJECTIONS: diagnose before responding. 'Already have a website' → assess the customer journey, not replacement. 'Already have an agency' → respect the relationship and identify only a real gap. 'Too expensive' → clarify budget vs value vs scope vs timing. 'Send info' → confirm the channel and whether one follow-up is welcome. 'Not interested' → at most one respectful clarification, then close. Repeated refusal or irritation → end immediately.\n\nDEMO TRANSPARENCY: Any homepage concept is an unsolicited private concept based on public information, not a commissioned/live website and not a change to their current website. Only offer it if it actually exists and the business meets selection criteria. If asked to remove it, do so before claiming completion.\n\nTECHNICAL / DELIVERY: Do not promise a CRM integration, API, platform compatibility, migration, hosting, security SLA, turnaround, case study, testimonial or result without verification. Use secure role-based account invitations; never request passwords by phone. Confirm page counts, one GBP location, competitor count, keyword caps, third-party fees, content approvals and post-launch support in writing.\n\nCONTACT PERMISSION: Ask before sending the audit / preview by email, WhatsApp or SMS. Permission to send one item does not mean permission for a campaign. Ask explicitly whether one follow-up call is welcome. Record channel, address, date, time and scope. Respect no-follow-up and Do Not Contact requests immediately. Commercial email and SMS require separate attention to applicable Australian Spam Act requirements, including consent, sender identification and unsubscribe.\n\nAUSTRALIAN TELEMARKETING COMPLIANCE: Cold calls to Australian numbers are subject to Australian rules even where the caller is offshore. Before calling, check the relevant Do Not Call Register requirements for the number and activity; mixed-use/personal numbers need special care. Business numbers primarily used for business are generally not eligible for registration, but this is not permission to ignore other rules. Telemarketing Industry Standard requirements include calling-time limits, caller identification, giving the caller's name/employer/reason, and ending the call when asked. Standard hours are weekdays 9am–8pm, Saturday 9am–5pm; no Sunday or national public holiday calls. Verify current ACMA / Do Not Call Register guidance and any consent or stricter applicable rules before operating. Display a return number. Caller must identify themselves and Digital Leads Co and explain the reason. If asked to stop, end and suppress. This playbook is operational guidance, not legal advice.\n\nREPLACE PLACEHOLDERS: [Business Name], [Name], [area], [GBP finding], [website finding], [URL], [service], [date], [time], [time zone], [approved address/channel], [return number]. All claims must be checked immediately before the call. Prospect-facing scripts are Australian English; internal guidance is Indonesian / English for the caller.",
